Used to actually throw an exception

throws
    ↓
Used in method declaration to indicate
that the method may throw an exception


The Rules of throws Propagation
-------------------------------
• Checked Exceptions: 
Java forces you to use the throws keyword if you want a checked exception (like IOException) to propagate. 
If you don't declare it or catch it, the code won't compile.

• Unchecked Exceptions: 
Unchecked exceptions (like NullPointerException or ArithmeticException) 
propagate automatically up the call stack without needing the throws keyword at all.

